# Week 1 — Calendar Project (GitHub + HTML/CSS/JS Portfolio Setup)

Welcome. This repo is your starting point for building a **real portfolio** while learning **GitHub the practical way**.

This week’s goal is simple:
- Learn the GitHub workflow by using it daily
- Build your first portfolio structure
- Build and deploy your **Calendar project**

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## What You Will Build (End of Week 1)

By the end of this week you must have:

1. A personal folder in this repo named after you  
2. Your own HTML entry page linked from the main `index.html`
3. Inside your folder:
   - A portfolio page that will list your projects
   - A `projects/` folder that contains your projects
4. Your first project inside `projects/`: **Calendar**
5. Everything pushed to GitHub with good commits

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Repo Structure (How This Works)

When you clone this repo, you will see:
- An `index.html` file (main entry for everyone)
- A folder containing starter material / examples (provided by the instructor)

You will create your own folder like this:

# Your personal entry page
/<your-name>/<your-name-Portfolio>.html 


# Your projects folder
/projects/calendar/
    index.html 
    styles.css

Example (for someone named "Sanje"):

/sanje/
    sanjesPortfolio.html
    /projects/
        /calendar/
            index.html
            styles.css


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Rules (Non-Negotiable)

- **Do not edit other people’s folders**
- Your folder name must be **your name** (lowercase, no spaces)
  - Example: `nhlakanipho`, `sanje`, `glory`
- All your work must be inside **your folder**
- Every project goes inside: `/<your-name>/projects/`

======================= CLONING THE REPOSISTORY AND GETTING TO WORK ========================

# Step-by-Step Instructions

## 1 Clone the Repo

### Option A: Using GitHub Desktop (recommended for beginners)
1. Open GitHub Desktop
2. Click **File → Clone repository**
3. Select the repo (or paste the URL)
4. Choose where to save it on your computer
5. Click **Clone**

### Option B: Using the Terminal
1. Open a terminal in the folder where you want the repo
2. Run:

git clone https://github.com/Utopianboy99/2026-development.git
cd 2026-development



# (2) Create Your Personal Folder

Inside the repo, create a folder with your name:
Example:

sanje/

glory/

emmanuel/

✅ Folder name rules:

lowercase only
no spaces
no special characters



# (3) Create Your Personal HTML Entry Page

Inside your folder, create:
<your-name-portfolio>.html

project folder :

projects/


# (4) Create Your Portfolio Page

<your-name>Portfolio.html

This page must:

Show your name
List your projects :

Link to your Calendar project (once you build it)
and other projects that you will build in the future



# (5) Create Your Projects Folder

Inside your folder, create:

projects/

Inside projects/, create your first project folder:

calendar/

So it becomes:

/<your-name>/projects/calendar/


# (6) Build the Calendar Project (Week 1 Work)

Inside:

/<your-name>/projects/calendar/

Create these files:
index.html
styles.css

This week your calendar must include:

✅ Calendar Requirements

- Uses HTML tables
- Uses basic CSS
- Displays days of the week
- Displays a month layout
- Has clean styling and readable spacing

# Bonus (if you finish early):

- Highlight today
- Buttons for next/previous month

==============Linking Your Page Into the Main index.html===========================

After you create your personal folder and portfolio.html, you must add your link to the main repo index.html.

Example:

<div class="person" id="sanje">
    <a href="sanje.html">
        Sanje
    </a>
</div>

<div class="person" id="hitlure">
    <a href="hitlure.html">
        Hitlure
    </a>
</div>

Add your link below the the last block, just like the examples provided above

## This allows anyone to open the repo and click into each student’s work.


======================= How to Save and Push Your Work (GitHub Workflow) =========================

# NB : Please make sure that you create your own branch first 
    Here's how you'll create your own branch

    (1) git checkout -b <name of your branch> 

    OR

    (2) git switch -c <name of your branch>

# Once you are done with that, you can do the following :

# (1) Check what changed
git status

# (2) Add changes
git add .

# (3) Add a commit message (Describe what changed in the code)
git commit -m "Add personal folder and portfolio structure"

# Good commit messages:

Add personal folder and portfolio structure
Create portfolio page with calendar link
Build calendar table layout
Style calendar grid

# Bad commit messages:

update
fix
changes

# (4) Push the changes

git push https://github.com/Utopianboy99/2026-development.git

# Common Mistakes (Avoid These)
- Putting your work outside your personal folder
- Forgetting to link your page in the main index.html
- Broken links due to wrong paths
- Using spaces or uppercase in folder names
- Huge commits with everything at once
